# Cloudflare Workers redirects. This site deploys with `npx wrangler deploy`,
# so wrangler parses this file at build time.
#
# STATUS CODES: wrangler validates every line and rejects anything that is not a
# real redirect status. `/* /404.html 404` is not valid here and fails the whole
# build, taking the deploy down with it. Only 301/302/303/307/308 belong below.
#
# There are two kinds of rule.
#
# 1. Named paths, sent to the matching section of the one-page site. These point
#    at equivalent content, which is what makes them ordinary redirects.
#
# 2. A catch-all at the very bottom, sending every unrecognised path to the home
#    page. This is the shop owner's decision, so that nobody who mistypes the
#    address reaches a dead end.
#
# ON THE CATCH-ALL, for whoever edits this next: Google treats a blanket
# redirect of missing pages to the home page as a "soft 404". What that costs in
# practice is that those URLs go unindexed and appear in Search Console under
# "Page with redirect" — it does NOT put duplicate copies of the home page into
# the index. Serving the home page's content at those paths instead
# (`/* /index.html 200`) is the version that WOULD do that, and it is the one
# genuinely worth avoiding. If this ever changes, keep it a 301; never make it a
# 200 rewrite.
#
# 404.html is still in the repo and reachable at /404. Nothing routes to it now,
# because this Worker returns a bare 404 for unmatched paths rather than serving
# a custom error document, and the catch-all below fires first anyway. It is kept
# for a host that does support one.

/home             /                301
/index            /                301

/contact          /#contact        301
/contact-us       /#contact        301
/hours            /#contact        301
